Makefile Notes --- Verilog / RTL Learning

1. Why Makefile?

When working with Verilog, the same commands are repeatedly typed:

iverilog -o sim.out design.v tb_design.v
vvp sim.out
gtkwave dump.vcd

A Makefile automates these commands so that one command can perform the
complete workflow.

Instead of manually doing:

iverilog ...
vvp ...
gtkwave ...

we can use:

make run PROJECT=Decoder_2to4

2. What is make?

make is a build automation tool.

It reads instructions from a file named:

Makefile

A Makefile contains targets and the commands that should execute for
those targets.

Basic structure:

target:
    command

Example:

hello:
    echo "Makefile is working!"

Run:

make hello

Output:

Makefile is working!

Important

The command under a target must begin with a TAB, not spaces.

Correct:

hello:
    echo "Hello"

Incorrect:

hello:
        echo "Hello"

To debug hidden characters:

cat -A Makefile

A real TAB appears as:

^I

3. Where does make look for Makefile?

By default, make searches the current working directory.

Our master Makefile is located at:

~/vlsi_workspace/verilog_Learning/Makefile

Therefore:

cd ~/vlsi_workspace/verilog_Learning
make ...

works.

If we are somewhere else, the current Makefile is not automatically
found.

Example:

cd ~
make

does not automatically search inside:

~/vlsi_workspace/verilog_Learning/

unless we explicitly specify the Makefile or create a more global
wrapper/command.

4. Makefile Targets

A target is a named operation.

Examples:

hello:
    echo "Master Makefile is working!"

make hello

Another example:

clean:
    rm -f sim.out

make clean

The target name is what comes after make.

5. Makefile Variables

Variables prevent repeatedly writing long paths.

Example:

PROJECT_DIR = RTL_Design/Combinational/Encoder/Priority_Encoder_4to2
RTL = $(PROJECT_DIR)/pri_enc_4to2.v
TB  = $(PROJECT_DIR)/tb_pri_enc_4to2.v
OUT = $(PROJECT_DIR)/sim.out
VCD = $(PROJECT_DIR)/dump.vcd

$(PROJECT_DIR) means:

Substitute the value stored in PROJECT_DIR.

For example:

$(PROJECT_DIR)/pri_enc_4to2.v

becomes:

RTL_Design/Combinational/Encoder/Priority_Encoder_4to2/pri_enc_4to2.v

Important distinction

This:

$(PROJECT_DIR)

is Make syntax.

Do not type it directly into Bash.

If you type:

cd $(PROJECT_DIR)

Bash tries to interpret PROJECT_DIR as a shell command/substitution,
because Bash does not know about Make variables.

The variable is expanded when you run:

make ...

6. TAB vs Spaces

This is one of the most important Makefile rules.

After:

target:

every command must start with a TAB.

Example:

priority_encoder:
    iverilog ...
    vvp sim.out
    gtkwave dump.vcd &

Not spaces.

If spaces are used, Make can produce:

*** missing separator (did you mean TAB instead of 8 spaces?).

Debug with:

cat -A Makefile

Look for:

^I

7. Icarus Verilog Flow

The basic Verilog simulation flow is:

RTL + Testbench
      |
      v
   iverilog
      |
      v
   sim.out
      |
      v
     vvp
      |
      v
   Simulation
      |
      v
   dump.vcd
      |
      v
   GTKWave

Compile

iverilog -o sim.out design.v tb_design.v

iverilog compiles the RTL and testbench.

-o sim.out specifies the output simulation executable.

Run

vvp sim.out

This executes the compiled simulation.

View waveform

gtkwave dump.vcd

GTKWave displays the waveform generated by the testbench.

8. Why cd matters for VCD files

Our testbenches commonly contain:

$dumpfile("dump.vcd");

This is a relative filename.

The VCD is created in the simulator's current working directory.

If simulation runs from:

~/vlsi_workspace/verilog_Learning

then:

$dumpfile("dump.vcd");

creates:

~/vlsi_workspace/verilog_Learning/dump.vcd

If simulation runs after:

cd RTL_Design/.../Priority_Encoder_4to2

then it creates:

RTL_Design/.../Priority_Encoder_4to2/dump.vcd

That is why our Makefile uses:

cd $(PROJECT_DIR) && vvp sim.out

9. Understanding &&

This:

cd $(PROJECT_DIR) && vvp sim.out

means:

Change into the project directory.

Only if cd succeeds, execute vvp sim.out.

Equivalent conceptually to:

cd project
    |
    | success?
    v
vvp sim.out

This is useful because the VCD then gets generated inside the project
directory.

10. Background execution with &

GTKWave is a GUI program.

In a Makefile:

gtkwave dump.vcd &

The & tells the shell to run GTKWave in the background.

Without &, the shell waits for GTKWave to finish.

With &, the terminal can continue.

11. clean Target

Generated files should not be confused with source files.

Source files:

design.v
tb_design.v

Generated files:

sim.out
dump.vcd

A clean target removes generated files:

clean:
    rm -f $(OUT)
    rm -f $(VCD)

rm -f means remove the file and do not complain if it does not exist.

For the whole repository, our Makefile uses find to remove generated
simulation artifacts.

12. Why we moved from manual targets to automatic discovery

Initially we had a target like:

priority_encoder:
    iverilog ...
    vvp ...
    gtkwave ...

That works, but it would require manually creating:

make priority_encoder
make decoder_2to4
make decoder_6to64
make mux_2to1
make mux_4to1
...

This becomes painful for a large RTL repository.

Instead, our master Makefile searches the project tree automatically.

Example:

make run PROJECT=Decoder_2to4

The Makefile finds the directory:

RTL_Design/Combinational/Decoders/Decoder_2to4

Then finds the RTL and testbench.

13. Repository structure

Our learning repository contains areas such as:

verilog_Learning/
├── README.md
├── Makefile
├── RTL_Design/
└── Verilog_Basics/

Examples of projects include:

RTL_Design/Combinational/Encoder/Priority_Encoder_4to2/

and:

RTL_Design/Combinational/Decoders/Decoder_2to4/

The Makefile searches:

SEARCH_DIRS = RTL_Design Verilog_Basics

So projects under those directories can be discovered automatically.

14. Important Linux commands used with Makefile

Show current directory

pwd

List files

ls

List detailed files

ls -l

Search files

find RTL_Design -type f

Search project directories

find RTL_Design -type d

Show Makefile

cat Makefile

Show hidden characters

cat -A Makefile

Edit Makefile

nano Makefile

Remove generated file

rm -f sim.out

15. Current Make commands

Our master Makefile provides commands such as:

make help

Shows available commands.

make list

Lists discoverable Verilog projects.

make run PROJECT=Decoder_2to4

Compile + simulate + open GTKWave.

make run PROJECT=Priority_Encoder_4to2

Same workflow for the priority encoder.

make compile PROJECT=Decoder_2to4

Compile only.

make sim PROJECT=Decoder_2to4

Run an already compiled sim.out.

make wave PROJECT=Decoder_2to4

Open an existing dump.vcd.

make lint PROJECT=Priority_Encoder_4to2

Run Verilator linting, provided Verilator is installed.

make clean

Remove generated simulation files.

make clean-all

Remove all supported generated simulation artifacts.

16. Current automated workflow

The main command:

make run PROJECT=<project>

performs approximately:

                 PROJECT NAME
                      |
                      v
               Find project directory
                      |
                      v
                 Find RTL .v
                      |
                      v
                Find tb_*.v
                      |
                      v
                   iverilog
                      |
                      v
                  sim.out
                      |
                      v
              cd into project
                      |
                      v
                   vvp
                      |
                      v
                  dump.vcd
                      |
                      v
                 GTKWave

17. Current limitation

The automatic Makefile is based on the repository convention:

project/
├── one main RTL .v
└── one tb_*.v

It may need modification for more complex projects.

For example:

FIFO/
├── fifo.v
├── fifo_memory.v
├── fifo_controller.v
└── tb_fifo.v

contains multiple RTL source files.

Likewise:

Project/
├── tb_test1.v
└── tb_test2.v

contains multiple testbenches.

These cases require more sophisticated project configuration.

Do not assume automation is magic. Automation works because the
repository follows conventions.

18. Makefile vs Bash

A useful distinction:

Bash

You directly execute commands:

iverilog ...
vvp ...
gtkwave ...

Make

You define reusable workflows:

run:
    iverilog ...
    vvp ...
    gtkwave ...

Then invoke:

make run

Make is therefore an automation/orchestration layer over command-line
tools.

19. Why this matters for RTL engineering

Makefiles are not an RTL language.

They are build and workflow automation.

In a real RTL project, you may eventually automate:

RTL
 |
 +--> simulation
 |
 +--> lint
 |
 +--> regression
 |
 +--> synthesis
 |
 +--> STA
 |
 +--> reports

The exact tools may change, but the principle remains:

one repeatable command
        |
        v
reproducible engineering flow

This becomes especially useful when projects become large.

20. Key lessons learned

You should now understand:

What a Makefile is.

What a target is.

How make target works.

Why commands require TAB indentation.

How Make variables work.

Difference between Make variables and Bash variables.

How iverilog compiles Verilog.

How vvp executes the compiled simulation.

How $dumpfile() determines the VCD location.

Why cd matters before running simulation.

What && does.

What & does.

How clean targets work.

How find can discover projects.

Why automatic discovery is better than manually defining dozens of
targets.

Why automatic discovery still has limitations.

How Make can become part of a larger RTL/ASIC automation flow.

21. The core commands to remember

For your current repository:

cd ~/vlsi_workspace/verilog_Learning

Then:

make help
make list
make run PROJECT=<project>
make compile PROJECT=<project>
make sim PROJECT=<project>
make wave PROJECT=<project>
make lint PROJECT=<project>
make clean

The most important one for daily RTL learning is:

make run PROJECT=<project>

Mental model

Do not memorize the Makefile line by line.

Remember this:

Makefile
   |
   +-- target = what I want to do
   |
   +-- variable = reusable value/path
   |
   +-- command = Linux tool to execute
   |
   +-- TAB = required command indentation
   |
   +-- make target = execute the workflow

And for simulation:

design.v + tb.v
       ↓
    iverilog
       ↓
    sim.out
       ↓
      vvp
       ↓
   simulation
       ↓
    dump.vcd
       ↓
    GTKWave

That is the knowledge you should carry forward---not just the giant
Makefile.
